# Question 3
**Answer this question in the following file:** `question3/q3.py`

**Description:** Using `sys.argv`, write a script that accepts multiple FASTA filenames as arguments and prints how many files will be processed. If no files are provided, print an error message.

<span style="color:#8b0153;">Hint</span>: To run this script, you will need to call it from the command line. We learned how to do this by calling something like the following:

  ```bash
  python script.py arg1 arg2
  ```

In practice, this only works if the script you want to run is in your current working directory. 

The chances are your current working directory is <span style="color:#4d9950;">COURSE_PRACTICE_QUESTIONS</span> since you were instructed to open this in VSCode. We have to tell the terminal where the script is located. We can either do this relative to the <span style="color:#4d9950;">COURSE_PRACTICE_QUESTIONS</span> directory (if this is your current directory - you can check where the folder prompt ends in your terminal) or you can provide the full path to the file. You can get either of these by right-clicking on the `q3.py` file in the file explorer and selecting **'Copy Path'** or **'Copy Relative Path'**. Once you have them, you can type the following, replacing the paths accordingly:


- **Full Path Example (<span style="color:#3d82ba;">Windows</span>):**
  ```bash
  python "C:\Users\name\example\path\coding\COURSE_PRACTICE_QUESTIONS\Chapter13_Creating_Executable_Python_Scripts\chapter13.1\question3\q3.py" file1.fasta file2.fasta
  ```

- **Full Path Example (<span style="color:#3d82ba;">macOS/Linux</span>):**
  ```bash
  python3 "/home/user/example/path/coding/COURSE_PRACTICE_QUESTIONS/Chapter13_Creating_Executable_Python_Scripts/chapter13.1/question3/q3.py" file1.fasta file2.fasta
  ```

- **Relative Path Example (from <span style="color:#4d9950;">COURSE_PRACTICE_QUESTIONS</span> - <span style="color:#3d82ba;">Windows</span>):**
  ```bash
  python Chapter13_Creating_Executable_Python_Scripts\chapter13.1\question3\q3.py file1.fasta file2.fasta
  ```

- **Relative Path Example (from <span style="color:#4d9950;">COURSE_PRACTICE_QUESTIONS</span> - <span style="color:#3d82ba;">macOS/Linux</span>):**
  ```bash
  python3 Chapter13_Creating_Executable_Python_Scripts/chapter13.1/question3/q3.py file1.fasta file2.fasta
  ```

<br>

# Question 4
**Answer this question in the following file:** `question4/q4.py`

**Description:** Create a sequence processing script that takes exactly two arguments: an input FASTA file and a minimum sequence length. Use `sys.argv` to access these arguments and print an error if the wrong number of arguments is provided. If the arguments are valid, print: **"Processing [filename] with minimum length of [length]"**.

<span style="color:#8b0153;">Hint</span>: To run this script, you'll need to specify its location in the command line. Use the same process as explained in **question 3** to get either the full path or relative path to `q4.py`. Remember, the script should be called with exactly two arguments:

